> We can compute the inverse CDFs and inverse complementary CDFs
> for most of these distributions through Boost's library.  My
> question was whether 80 bins was enough to specialize the code
> to use the complemnentary CDFs for p > 0.5.

Oh. Well, in R they are the same to at least 13 digits of precision

qchisq(ppoints(80), df = 79) - qchisq(1 - ppoints(80), df = 79, lower.tail = FALSE)

so I doubt it would make any difference to the tests.

Ben

