Now that we're on the topic, I've been meaning to ask if
the way the leapfrog is formulated in terms of half step -
full step - half step is for reversibility?

I've also never understood the "reversing the momentum"
business.  I realize that if you reverse the momentum, then
you should retrace your steps backward if you have a perfect
integrator.  But in practice, we don't have a perfect integrator.
And in practice, we generate a new momentum each time in practice.

- Bob

On 3/7/13 1:03 PM, Matt Hoffman wrote:
> Volume preservation is a soft constraint in an MCMC transitionï¿½you can

> still adjust for it in the Metropolis correction if you can compute
> the determinants of the Jacobians of every transition. (Cf. the recent
> Lan et al. paper on Lagrangian Monte Carlo.) But reversibility is a

> hard constraintï¿½you can't satisfy detailed balance without it. The

> leapfrog integrator is both volume preserving and reversible, which
> makes it easy to prove correctness in HMC. (Ditto for the generalized
> leapfrog, assuming the fixed-point iterations converge.)
>
> Matt
