Which elements of the style?  I tried to stay consistent with the style in the rest of the files, save for some sparse white space here and there which I had to throw in to quickly identify different blocks of code, and I'm happy to fix things up.

I did shed a tear every time I had to delete a linebreak between a if/for and the opening brace.

> What's the acceptance statistic and why is it part of a sample?
> Isn't acceptance a property of a proposal sample relative to
> a previous sample?

If we think of each "sample" as actually a transition in a Markov chain then the acceptance statistic (acceptance probability in the Metropolis case, tree-sampling probability in the NUTS case) is an important element of the sample.  On a more practical side, it's important to pass along for adaptation and helpful for diagnostics (for example right now we only implicitly use the mean of this statistic, assuming that the stepsize adaptation has converged, but the variance can also be useful in diagnosing wacky geometries).

> I'm ok with using "cont"/"disc" instead of "real"/"int",
> but it should be consistent.  (Yes, I realize it's all discrete
> floating  point reps underneath for reals, but Andrew 1 didn't
> like "double", which is how I originally had it.)

cont/disc seemed much more natural without adding too many characters.  Was there anywhere in the mcmc code that I failed to keep things consistent, or are you referring to the model code?

> Would it be useful to generalize away from teh normal
> distribution for generating momenta?  (I realize a fatter
> tailed proposal doesn't help much, but it seems an easy
> thing to generalize if it'll ever be needed for eval, etc.)

This was one place where I compromised because I didn't fully understand Boost's RNG and I couldn't imagine anyone trying non-quadratic kinetic energies anytime soon.  I'd like to keep the Hamiltonians stateless, so would this be acceptable?

- Pass the rand_int to sample_p by reference.
