> If we think of each "sample" as actually a transition in a Markov chain then the acceptance statistic (acceptance probability in the Metropolis case, tree-sampling probability in the NUTS case) is an important element of the sample.  On a more practical side, it's important to pass along for adaptation and helpful for diagnostics (for example right now we only implicitly use the mean of this statistic, assuming that the stepsize adaptation has converged, but the variance can also be useful in diagnosing wacky geometries).

That's what I suspected.

Conceptually, I don't think an acceptance probability is
part of a sample per se.  As you point out, its interpretation
and contents is going to be very sampler specific.  If we
had an exact sampler then there wouldn't even be an acceptance
